{"name":"Hot Dog Relish","hex":"#717c3e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hot","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Hote."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Relating to heat and conditions which produce it.\n Having or giving off a high temperature.","Having or giving off a high temperature."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hot"},{"term":"dog","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mammal of the family Canidae:\n The species Canis familiaris (sometimes designated Canis lupus familiaris), domesticated for thousands of years and of highly variable appearance because of human breeding.","The species Canis familiaris (sometimes designated Canis lupus familiaris), domesticated for thousands of years and of highly variable appearance because of human breeding."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To pursue with the intent to catch.","To follow in an annoying or harassing way."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dog"},{"term":"relish","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Flavour or taste;  an instance of this.\n\n\n Followed by for: one's liking or taste for something; a fondness.\n\n A small amount; a tinge, a trace.","Flavour or taste;  an instance of this."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To give (something) (a pleasant) flavour or taste; to make appetizing. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}\n\n To take delight or pleasure in (someone or oneself, or something).","To give (something) (a pleasant) flavour or taste; to make appetizing. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/relish"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hot dog","blocks":[{"text":"A hot dog is a grilled, steamed, or boiled sausage served in the slit of a partially sliced bun. The term hot dog can also refer to the sausage itself. The sausage used is a wiener or a frankfurter. The names of these sausages commonly refer to their assembled dish. Hot dog preparation and condiments vary worldwide. Common condiments include mustard, ketchup, relish, onions in tomato sauce, and cheese sauce. Other toppings include sauerkraut, jalapeños, chili, grated cheese, coleslaw, bacon and olives. Hot dog variants include the corn dog and pigs in a blanket."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Hot_dog_with_mustard.png/330px-Hot_dog_with_mustard.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hot_dog","wikidata":"Q181055"},{"title":"Dog","blocks":[{"text":"The dog is a domesticated descendant of wolves. Also called the domestic dog, it was selectively bred during the Late Pleistocene by hunter-gatherers. Dogs and the modern gray wolf share a common ancestor. Dogs were the first species to be domesticated over 14,000 years ago, before the development of agriculture, though genetic studies suggest the domestication process may have begun over 25,000 years ago. Due to their long association with humans, dogs have gained the ability to thrive on a starch-rich diet that would be inadequate for other canids."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Huskiesatrest.jpg/330px-Huskiesatrest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dog","wikidata":"Q144"},{"title":"Relish","blocks":[{"text":"A relish is a cooked and pickled culinary dish made of chopped vegetables, fruits or herbs, typically used as a condiment to enhance a staple. Examples are chutneys and the North American relish, a pickled cucumber jam eaten with hot dogs. In North America, the word \"relish\" is frequently used to describe a single variety of finely chopped pickled cucumber relish, such as pickle, dill and sweet relishes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Nsima_Relishes.JPG/330px-Nsima_Relishes.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Relish","wikidata":"Q766777"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Frankfurter_stand_LOC_det.4a13502.jpg/500px-Frankfurter_stand_LOC_det.4a13502.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Frankfurter_stand_LOC_det.4a13502.jpg/1280px-Frankfurter_stand_LOC_det.4a13502.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Frankfurter_stand_LOC_det.4a13502.jpg/500px-Frankfurter_stand_LOC_det.4a13502.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Carts selling frankfurters in New York City, c. 1906. The price is listed as \"3 cents each or 2 for 5 cents\".","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFrankfurter_stand_LOC_det.4a13502.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Indoor_Sports_-_Listening_to_Two_Gay_Dogs_Kid_One_Another_about_Their_First_Frankfurter_at_the_Six_Day_Race.jpg/500px-Indoor_Sports_-_Listening_to_Two_Gay_Dogs_Kid_One_Another_about_Their_First_Frankfurter_at_the_Six_Day_Race.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Indoor_Sports_-_Listening_to_Two_Gay_Dogs_Kid_One_Another_about_Their_First_Frankfurter_at_the_Six_Day_Race.jpg/1280px-Indoor_Sports_-_Listening_to_Two_Gay_Dogs_Kid_One_Another_about_Their_First_Frankfurter_at_the_Six_Day_Race.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Indoor_Sports_-_Listening_to_Two_Gay_Dogs_Kid_One_Another_about_Their_First_Frankfurter_at_the_Six_Day_Race.jpg/500px-Indoor_Sports_-_Listening_to_Two_Gay_Dogs_Kid_One_Another_about_Their_First_Frankfurter_at_the_Six_Day_Race.jpg?utm_s